  <NugetDependency Id="LVlLiyDrifBuWIgoQ7tPAt" Location="VL.GeoJSON" Version="0.0.0" />
  <Patch Id="QrK0B2ArLCRdkFzNSzPi2k">
    <Canvas Id="I7h20LcrpSPPFcWQj5LnZ1" DefaultCategory="Main" CanvasType="FullCategory" />
    <!--

    ************************ Application ************************

-->
    <Node Name="Application" Bounds="100,100" Id="Rk56OliWUTWPWpBjZzSWK6">
      <p:NodeReference>
        <Choice Kind="ContainerDefinition" Name="Process" />
        <FullNameCategoryReference ID="Primitive" />
      </p:NodeReference>
      <Patch Id="Va76RXeZbJhUahxnDRLHM6">
        <Canvas Id="PvCDE59mmqYipEr7wbN1c1" CanvasType="Group">
          <Pad Id="Ghem1evZos8wBjK0az903A" Bounds="-820,-120,660,1560" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Prompt - Which door.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;A building with three doors, a few streets, and your cursor. Point somewhere in the town.&#xD;&#xA;One door lights up orange and a teal route leaves it: that is the door to walk out of if you&#xD;&#xA;want to reach the cursor by the streets. LEAVE BY names it; ALL DOORS (m) shows what the&#xD;&#xA;other two would have cost.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;PUT THE CURSOR TOP-LEFT, by the dead end of West Avenue. The north door is nearest as the&#xD;&#xA;crow flies - and the patch says South door. North Street has no west half: from the north&#xD;&#xA;door you would walk east, down East Avenue, back along South Street and up. From the south&#xD;&#xA;door you walk west and up. Nearest is not quickest. Chapter 13 said so with one dot; here the&#xD;&#xA;question is asked three times a frame and the answers are compared.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;THE THIN GREY STUBS are honest. No door is on a street. ShortestPath snaps a door to the&#xD;&#xA;nearest street END, and DOOR TO STREET (m) says how far that was - 20, 40 or 30 m here - and&#xD;&#xA;it is added to the route before the doors are compared. The other stub joins your cursor to&#xD;&#xA;its own nearest end. Read the two stubs as 'this is where the network thinks you are'.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;WHERE THE TOWN COMES FROM. Not typed into a box this time: help\Assets\which-door.geojson,&#xD;&#xA;one file, thirteen features. Read GeoJSON hands back all of them and the patch sorts them by&#xD;&#xA;what they ARE - LineStrings become streets and go into BuildNetwork, the Polygon is drawn as&#xD;&#xA;the building, the Points are doors. GeometryType asks; three Keep loops answer. The name on&#xD;&#xA;each door is a property, read back with TryGetValue when a door wins.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;AND THE FILE IS LYING, ON PURPOSE. GeoJSON has no way to say 'metres' - RFC 7946 says the&#xD;&#xA;numbers ARE longitude and latitude, full stop. These are metres in a sketch, so every feature&#xD;&#xA;carries a 'units' property admitting it, and the patch declares Metres per scene unit = 300&#xD;&#xA;exactly as chapter 13 did. A real projected dataset would come as GeoPackage or Shapefile&#xD;&#xA;with a coordinate system attached. This is a sketch that borrowed a format, and it says so.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;NETWORKS BUILT reads 1 and stays there: the file is parsed once (Parses: 1), the same street&#xD;&#xA;objects arrive every frame, and BuildNetwork sees no change. Three routes are recomputed&#xD;&#xA;every frame and are not counted - on a town this size that costs nothing.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;MAKE IT YOURS - some of these, none of these, or something else entirely:&#xD;&#xA;  Add a fourth door. One more Point feature in the file, any name; the patch does not count.&#xD;&#xA;  Give North Street its west half back - [[-350, 150], [0, 150]] - and watch the top-left&#xD;&#xA;  corner change its mind.&#xD;&#xA;  Move a door 5 m off its lane end. DOOR TO STREET grows by 5, and so does its total.&#xD;&#xA;  Delete the East Lane feature. The east door still snaps - to the wrong corner, and the&#xD;&#xA;  stub shows you exactly how wrong. That is what the number is for.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;You don't have to follow the prompt exactly. Or even at all.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;THE HONEST GAP: BuildNetwork and ShortestPath are EXPERIMENTAL and say so in their category.&#xD;&#xA;NetTopologySuite ships no shortest path; these were written for chapter 13, and this prompt&#xD;&#xA;is the second thing to want them unchanged - which is part of how it will be decided whether&#xD;&#xA;they deserve a permanent name. Snapping goes to street ENDS, not to the nearest point along a&#xD;&#xA;street; a door halfway down a long block would snap to its far corner. A real network&#xD;&#xA;toolkit splits the edge. This one tells you the distance instead.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Data: hand-drawn for this package, MIT like the patch. Uses VL.GeoJSON and VL.NetTopologySuite.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">11</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <!-- ===== A. the file, and three loops that sort it by what each feature IS ===== -->
          <Pad Id="TNGf5HMmp9sdhYuN1DvHIi" Bounds="60,-40,700,15" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="A. ONE FILE, THIRTEEN FEATURES - sorted by GeometryType into streets, a building and doors.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">11</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
